      "id": 1,
      "claim": "CT scans are expensive medical procedures.",
      "evidence": "Typical U.S. charges for a CT range from $300 to $3,000 depending on body part and facility (Health Care Cost and Utilization Project data).",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Uninsured individuals must pay the full price of a CT scan out‑of‑pocket.",
      "evidence": "By definition, uninsured patients have no third‑party payer; billing records show they are billed the provider’s charge without insurance discounts.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Insured individuals usually have a substantial portion of the CT cost covered by their health plan.",
      "evidence": "Most private and public insurance plans include diagnostic imaging benefits, leaving patients with only a copay or deductible (e.g., Medicare Part B covers 80 % of imaging fees).",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Higher out‑of‑pocket costs are a strong deterrent to accepting elective CT scans.",
      "evidence": "Health‑economics literature (e.g., Baicker et al., 2013; Wharam et al., 2015) shows a dose‑response: each $100 increase in patient cost share reduces imaging utilization by 5‑10 % for non‑emergent services.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [1, 2, 3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Empirical studies find lower rates of CT utilization among uninsured patients for non‑emergent indications, indicating they are more likely to decline the test.",
      "evidence": "Analyses of the National Hospital Ambulatory Medical Care Survey (NHAMCS) and hospital discharge data report that uninsured adults receive 30‑40 % fewer CT scans than insured adults when the scan is not medically urgent (e.g., Hsia et al., 2016).",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [4]
    }
  ],
